 The `cruft diff` command optionally accepts an `--exit-code` flag that will make cruft exit with a non-0 code if any diff is found. You can combine this flag with the `skip` section of your `.cruft.json` to make stricter CI checks that ensures any improvement to the template is always submitted upstream.
 
+## Ignoring files with .cruftignore
+
+Sometimes a template ships files that are meant to be owned by the generated
+project: local configuration, environment specific settings, generated assets
+that users are expected to customize. Updating those files from the template
+would clobber the user's changes, and `cruft diff` would keep flagging them
+forever.
+
+Template authors can declare such paths in a `.cruftignore` file placed at the
+root of the generated project (that is, inside the template's top-level
+directory so it gets rendered like any other file). The file uses the same
+pattern syntax as `.gitignore`:
+
+```
+# Local configuration is owned by the project, never touch it on update.
+config.ini
+settings/
+
+# Logs are ignored everywhere, except the one we want to keep in sync.
+*.log
+!keep.log
+```
+
+Once a `.cruftignore` is present in the template rendering being compared:
+
+* `cruft update` never creates, modifies or deletes an ignored path. The
+  file on disk always wins, no `*.rej` conflict files are produced for
+  ignored paths, and each ignored path that would otherwise have changed is
+  reported as `Skipping ignored path: <path>`.
+* `cruft diff` does not report differences for ignored paths, and they do
+  not influence the `--exit-code` outcome.
+* `cruft create` is not affected: every template file, including the
+  `.cruftignore` file itself, is rendered as usual.
+
+The rules that apply are always the ones shipped by the template version
+being compared (for `cruft update`, the new template version). The
+`.cruftignore` file itself is never ignored: it follows the normal update
+rules, so template authors can evolve the rule set over time and have it
+propagate to generated projects.
+
+Patterns follow `.gitignore` conventions: blank lines and `#` comments are
+skipped, a leading `!` negates a pattern, a trailing `/` matches directories
+only, patterns containing a `/` are anchored to the project root while
+patterns without one match the basename at any depth, `*` and `?` never
+cross a directory boundary, and `**` matches across directories. When
+several patterns match, the last one wins. Like in git, a file below an
+excluded directory cannot be re-included by a later negation.
+
+
